@anon.0de5ee8f4a9046f07397637c35d34c13.2171 = private unnamed_addr constant [1439 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for `printf`-style string formatting, and offers to replace it with\0A`str.format` calls.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0A`printf`-style string formatting has a number of quirks, and leads to less\0Areadable code than using `str.format` calls or f-strings. In general, prefer\0Athe newer `str.format` and f-strings constructs over `printf`-style string\0Aformatting.\0A\0A## Example\0A\0A```python\0A\22%s, %s\22 % (\22Hello\22, \22World\22)  # \22Hello, World\22\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A\0A```python\0A\22{}, {}\22.format(\22Hello\22, \22World\22)  # \22Hello, World\22\0A```\0A\0A```python\0Af\22{'Hello'}, {'World'}\22  # \22Hello, World\22\0A```\0A\0A## Fix safety\0A\0AIn cases where the format string contains a single generic format specifier\0A(e.g. `%s`), and the right-hand side is an ambiguous expression,\0Awe cannot offer a safe fix.\0A\0AFor example, given:\0A\0A```python\0A\22%s\22 % val\0A```\0A\0A`val` could be a single-element tuple, _or_ a single value (not\0Acontained in a tuple). Both of these would resolve to the same\0Aformatted string when using `printf`-style formatting, but\0Aresolve differently when using f-strings:\0A\0A```python\0Aval = 1\0Aprint(\22%s\22 % val)  # \221\22\0Aprint(\22{}\22.format(val))  # \221\22\0A\0Aval = (1,)\0Aprint(\22%s\22 % val)  # \221\22\0Aprint(\22{}\22.format(val))  # \22(1,)\22\0A```\0A\0A## References\0A- [Python documentation: `printf`-style String Formatting](https://docs.python.org/3/library/stdtypes.html#old-string-formatting)\0A- [Python documentation: `str.format`](https://docs.python.org/3/library/stdtypes.html#str.format)\0A", align 1
