@anon.0de5ee8f4a9046f07397637c35d34c13.2182 = private unnamed_addr constant [837 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for uses of `bin(...).count(\221\22)` to perform a population count.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0AIn Python 3.10, a `bit_count()` method was added to the `int` class,\0Awhich is more concise and efficient than converting to a binary\0Arepresentation via `bin(...)`.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Ax = bin(123).count(\221\22)\0Ay = bin(0b1111011).count(\221\22)\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Ax = (123).bit_count()\0Ay = 0b1111011.bit_count()\0A```\0A\0A## Fix safety\0AThis rule's fix is marked as unsafe unless the argument to `bin` can be inferred as\0Aan instance of a type that implements the `__index__` and `bit_count` methods because this can\0Achange the exception raised at runtime for an invalid argument.\0A\0A## Options\0A- `target-version`\0A\0A## References\0A- [Python documentation:`int.bit_count`](https://docs.python.org/3/library/stdtypes.html#int.bit_count)\0A", align 1
